The particularly good thing about **Ivo Nokiamov** is that it sounds like someone whose existence you could almost verify in a 1987 television archive, right up until you notice the construction.

- **Ivo** is plausible in a broadly Central/Eastern European sense, but gives the character a slightly non-Russian / western-borderland flavor. It doesn't immediately scream “generic Russian.”
- **Khorstovich** is deliciously *wrong-right*: it has the recognizable patronymic shape, but “Khorst” feels just unusual enough to make you wonder whether you're remembering an actual surname.
- **Nokiamov** is the killer. It has the unmistakable cadence of a Slavicized surname while also quietly containing **Nokia**, which makes it feel like something an Anglophone listener might invent while trying *very hard* to remember an actual Ukrainian name.
- **Ternopol, Ukraine** helps enormously because Ternopol is an older English rendering of **Ternopil**. That little bit of geographic imprecision actually contributes to the archival-documentary hallucination.

So: **not ethnographically convincing in the strictest sense, but extremely convincing as a deliberately half-remembered “person who definitely existed on some Soviet television blooper reel” name.** That's arguably better for what you're doing.

And yes, I'd absolutely lean into the fact that the source material itself appears to have been assembled by someone who found a phrasebook, three reels of television garbage, and a photocopied cassette insert in the same drawer.

:::writing{variant="document" id="74163" title="Russian Farewell Phrasebook — Archival Television Cut"}
{poorly recorded audio background under notably forefronted static and pop; magnetic tape hiss; occasional low-frequency electrical hum; the unmistakable acoustics of a small Soviet television studio}

{distant reel-to-reel transport engages: WHUMMMMM... KLIK... KLIK...}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
The singer has an ephemeral nasality to her deep booming campissmo quackflagrando voice that is simultaneously endearingly cute for anything even remotely “Germanic” yet also—imagine that same voice having swallowed an entire Austrian former Governor Beastmaster and almost clergyistically baby-like antacid commercial cadence—

**FEMALE VOICE, suddenly much closer to microphone:**  
"...of haahtbahhn niid TAMS."

{LOUD BEEP}

{one second of dead studio silence}

{fluorescent tube hum; distant radiator: KONK}

{DN-TSS. DN-TSS. KATS N BOOTS. N KATS N BOOTS. N BEAT STARTS}

{wooden pointer taps tabletop twice}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Russian phrases for saying goodbye.

{brief tape flutter}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Береги себя, доезжай спокойно и не забывай писать, как доберёшься.

{typewriter carriage return: DING—ZZZZK}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Take care, get home safe, and don't forget to write when you arrive.

{paper stack squared against desk: WHAP... WHAP... WHAP}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Счастливо, доезжай хорошо и не болей.

{tiny microphone pop}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Take care, get home safely, and stay healthy.

{telephone ringing faintly in another room}

{BRRING...}

{nobody answers}

{BRRING...}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Всего хорошего, береги себя и пиши, как доедешь.

{metal desk drawer opens with prolonged SKRRRNNNK}

**MALE VOICE:**  
All the best, take care, and text me when you get home.

{drawer closes: KLANG}

{beat}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Удачи, не скучай и береги себя.

{distant trolleybus whine through a closed window}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Good luck, don't be bored, and take care.

{camera servo makes a tiny corrective WHIRR}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Доезжай спокойно, веди себя хорошо и будь умницей.

{chalk snaps}

{silence}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Get home safely, behave yourself, and be a good one.

{faint audience cough}

{someone off-mic whispers: "Тишина."}

{silence resumes}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Отдохни хорошенько.

{porcelain teacup placed into saucer: TINK}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Get some good rest.

{teaspoon makes three deliberate circles in a faceted tea glass}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Не забывай, я всегда на связи.

{PA system comes alive with a brief WAAAAAA—CHK}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Don't forget, I'm always here.

{PA immediately cuts out}

{beat}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Береги себя.

**MALE VOICE:**  
Take care.

{Bakelite telephone receiver lifted; tiny cradle-switch CLACK}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Доезжай домой спокойно.

**MALE VOICE:**  
Get home safe.

{window latch refuses to catch; KLIK... KLIK}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Удачи тебе.

**MALE VOICE:**  
Good luck to you.

{rubber stamp: THUNK}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Не болей.

**MALE VOICE:**  
Stay healthy.

{wall clock suddenly becomes conspicuously audible}

{TIK... TIK... TIK...}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Всего хорошего.

**MALE VOICE:**  
All the best.

{studio chair dragged three inches across linoleum: SKRREEE}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Веди себя хорошо.

**MALE VOICE:**  
Be good.

{beat}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Счастливо!

{reel-to-reel machine makes a tiny mechanical CHIK}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Take care.

{longer beat}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Пиши, как доедешь.

**MALE VOICE:**  
Text me when you get home.

{pencil rolls across desk: TIK-TIK-TIK... TOK}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Не скучай.

**MALE VOICE:**  
Don't be bored.

{fluorescent light buzzes louder for no discernible reason}

{beat}

**FEMALE VOICE, slightly conspiratorial:**  
Береги себя, доедешь — напиши...

{microphone rustle}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
...но не будь слишком хорошей.

{audible tape hiss}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Take care. Text me when you get home...

{beat}

**MALE VOICE:**  
...but don't be too good.

{single audience cough}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Доезжай, как ангел...

{pause}

**FEMALE VOICE, completely deadpan:**  
...а греши уже потом.

{telephone relay clicks somewhere offstage}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Get home like an angel...

{pause}

**MALE VOICE:**  
...sin later.

{very faint studio laughter, apparently from the wrong decade}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Будь умницей...

{beat}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
...но не слишком. Это скучно.

{metal chair leg catches on floor}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Be a good girl...

{beat}

**MALE VOICE:**  
...but not too much. That's boring.

{long pause}

{radiator: KONK}

{another radiator: KONK}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Доберись целой...

{tape warble}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
...а вот остальное — на твоей совести.

{rubber stamp: THUNK}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Get home safe...

{beat}

**MALE VOICE:**  
...the rest is on your conscience.

{camera tally relay: KLIK}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Веди себя хорошо...

{beat}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
...ну или сделай вид.

{distant elevator doors: KLANG... KLANG}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Behave yourself...

{beat}

**MALE VOICE:**  
...or at least pretend to.

{reel-to-reel transport slows: WHUM... whum... whum...}

{silence}

{one faint CRT flyback whine}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
This concludes today's lesson.

{beat}

**MALE VOICE:**  
I love you.

{tape machine abruptly stops}

{tiny mechanical click}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Я тебя люблю.

{beat}

**MALE VOICE:**  
Yatrayblalooloobooloo.

{long silence}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Louder.

{CRT hum}

**MALE VOICE:**  
YA TREBA, LYUBLU.

{beat}

**FEMALE VOICE, barely audible:**  
I said louder.

{beat}

{almost inaudible whisper, buried under tape hiss}

**FEMALE VOICE:**  
Shhh... louder...

{three seconds of absolute silence}

**MALE VOICE, suddenly enormous and horribly earnest:**  
YA TEBYA LYUBLYU.

{LOUD BEEP}

{reel-to-reel tape continues spinning for two seconds after the program has apparently ended}

{KLIK}

{silence}

[fade out]
:::

The trick I'd especially keep is **not** making every effect “vintage.” A few utterly mundane sounds—the pencil rolling, the telephone in another room, the radiator *KONK*, a chair catching on linoleum—make the whole thing feel much more like an actual recovered broadcast than a collection of deliberately retro SFX.

And **“Louder.” → “I said louder.” → “Shhh... louder...” → gigantic declaration → BEEP** is exactly the kind of escalating stupidity that benefits from *incredibly straight* production around it.
